      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T18:48:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The byte-overlap counting looks right to me. A VMA that starts or ends partway through a 16KB page only counts its own slices. Two 4KB VMAs that share a swapped 16KB page split it between them, rather than both counting all of it. And rounding `xa_index` down handles a large entry that starts before `start`.\n\nOnly 4KB VMAs use it, though, so would you mind moving it out of `mm/shmem.c` into a P3S header? For example, it could be a static inline in a new `include/linux/p3s/shmem.h` that takes the VMA and works out the range with `vma_file_offset_at()`:\n\n```\nstatic inline unsigned long vma_shmem_swap_usage(struct vm_area_struct *vma,\n\t\t\t\t\t\t unsigned long start,\n\t\t\t\t\t\t unsigned long end)\n```\n\nIt only needs `i_pages` and the xarray API. `shmem_swap_usage()` and `smaps_pte_hole()` would then each need only a short call (see the comments there), and the `extern` prototype in `\u003clinux/shmem_fs.h\u003e` that checkpatch flags could go.\n\nThe new `#include \u003clinux/p3s/vma.h\u003e` lines in `mm/shmem.c` and `fs/proc/task_mmu.c` aren\u0027t needed in either case: `\u003clinux/mm_types.h\u003e` already includes that header through `\u003clinux/p3s.h\u003e`. That is how `task_mmu.c` uses `vma_file_offset()` today.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "ee1c29f8a67aab111e604a51523618a37698f233",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
